33

The number of tests `npm test` runs in server/sfu. One definition, read
by the CI step "Run the call server's tests, and count them" and by
nothing else.

Measured: `node --test --test-reporter=tap test/*.test.js` reports
`# tests 33`, which is 23 in protocol.test.js plus 10 in token.test.js,
subtests inside a `describe` included and the four suites not counted.

## Why there is a floor at all

`- run: npm test` was the whole step. The package script is

    node --test test/*.test.js

and on node v22.22.2, over a glob that matches NOTHING, that prints

    1..0
    # tests 0
    # pass 0
    # fail 0

and EXITS 0. So emptying `server/sfu/test/`, renaming the files out of
`*.test.js`, or moving the directory takes all 33 assertions about the
call server's protocol and token handling with it, and the job goes
green. Not a hypothetical: it was probed, in that shell, before this
file was written.

A file that still exists but defines no tests is reported as `# tests 1`
-- node counts the file itself when it holds nothing. So two emptied
files read as 2, not 0, which is still a long way under this floor.

## Why the reporter is pinned

The step reads `# tests N`, which is the TAP reporter's summary. node
chooses `tap` when stdout is not a terminal and `spec` when it is, and
`spec` spells the same line `i tests 33`. A count that depends on
whether something is a terminal is a count that changes when the runner
does, so `--test-reporter=tap` is in the package script rather than
left to the default. It is why `npm test` prints TAP by hand as well.

## Raising and lowering it

Raise it freely, in the commit that adds the tests. Lower it only with
the deleted tests named in the commit message.
